  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 119 PM EDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\n***** THURSDAY THROUGH THE WEEKEND *****\n\nGlobal models and their respective ensembles agree that an expansive \ntrough will move into the area from the Northern Plains and Upper \nMidwest late Thursday into Friday. Southwest flow ahead of this \nsystem will bring deeper moisture and a slightly warmer airmass to \nthe Bluegrass state on Thursday, when NBM advertises a 30-60% chance \nhigh temperatures surpass 90 degrees along and west of I-65 (highest \nchances south of US 62). Precipitation chances roughly split the \narea during the day, as the incoming trough axis remains upstream \nacross IL and the aforementioned cutoff low gets pushed \nnortheastward into the Mid-Atlantic. However, Thursday night some \ndecaying convection may enter northern portions of the area.\n\nStorm chances increase area wide by the end of the work week as a \ncouple shortwaves rounding the base of the upper level trough swing \nthrough the region. Uncertainty surrounds the degree of \ndestabilization that may materialize each day due to potential for \ncloud cover, but LREF mean wind shear runs at only 10-20 kt through \nthe weekend which should limit storm organization. Still, pop up \nstorms each afternoon and evening are a risk, with the highest \nchances (40-60%) on Friday. Any storm could generate locally heavy \nrain given PWATs approaching 2 inches, slow storm motion, and a deep \n(> 13kft) warm cloud layer, but the probability of severe weather \n- at least at this juncture - appears low (less than 15%).\n\nAround the end of the forecast period, uncertainty increases in the \nupper level pattern, with a dipole in Empirical Orthogonal Function \n(EOF) 1 for 500mb heights suggesting this is primarily driven by \nmodel variance in when ridging over the Intermountain West spreads \neast into the Ohio Valley/Great Lakes. From cluster analysis, 2 \nbroad solutions become apparent by Sunday-Monday: ~45% of long range \nensemble members depict ridging fully over KY/IN by Monday, favoring \nheat indices in the triple digits and minimal chances for \nprecipitation; meanwhile the remaining members (55%) lean towards a \nmore zonal/northwest flow solution that would result in slightly \nmilder temperatures (mid 80s) and keep a risk for scattered storms \naround, especially north of I-64. WPC's day 8-14 probabilistic \nhazards outlook highlights the local area with a slight risk for \nboth heavy rainfall and extreme heat.",
